  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 931 PM MST Sat Nov 7 2026\n\nThe string of mostly sunny and dry days will stretch well into \nnext week as we continue to dry out from an extraordinary wet\nand cool end of October. The 08/00Z H500 hand analysis map is\nshowing a tilted Rex block over the West/Southwest. The blocking\nhigh in this pattern has forced the jet and any associated\nmoisture well to our North. This ridge will hold strong as it\nelongates across the Intermountain West to the Central High\nPlains through early next week. Temperatures will remain on the\nhigh side of normal in most areas through this time. The \ndesert valleys...either due to soggy soils or strong low level \ninversions...will run closer to normal. The inversions and\ntrapped moisture in the planetary boundary layer could lead to\nsome fog in some of the valleys...but this is a low confidence\nforecast. A northern stream wave dropping out of the Northern \nRockies to the High Plains by mid week will force the ridge back\nto the West. This will have minimal impact to our sensible \nweather and persistence to our warm and dry forecast continues. \nThe ridge looks to be forced once again back into the Rockies by\nthe end of the week as a closed low replaces it back to the \nWest over eastern Pacific.",
